==============================================================================
0) BIG PICTURE FIRST — WHY REPLICATION EVEN EXISTS?
==============================================================================

Before jumping into Single Leader / Multi Leader / Leaderless, interviewer ko
sabse pehle ye dikhna chahiye ki tum "WHY" samajhte ho.

Replication = same data ko multiple machines / nodes / data centers / regions
me copy karke rakhna.

Simple real life analogy:
Imagine tumhare paas ek important notebook hai.

Case 1:
Notebook sirf ek jagah rakhi.
→ Agar vo kho gayi = data lost.

Case 2:
Notebook ki 3 copies bana di.
→ Ek ghar pe
→ Ek office me
→ Ek friend ke paas

Ab:
- ek copy destroy ho jaye → still safe
- multiple log padh sake → faster reads
- dusre city me log quickly access kare → lower latency

THIS IS EXACTLY REPLICATION.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORE GOALS OF REPLICATION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Durability
2) Throughput / Scalability
3) Geography / Low Latency / DR
4) Availability / Fault Tolerance
5) Analytics isolation
6) Backup & recovery safety

Interview line:
"We replicate data primarily for durability, availability, read scalability,
geo-latency reduction, and disaster recovery."

==============================================================================
1) DURABILITY — WHY?
==============================================================================

Durability = once write acknowledged, data should survive crashes.

Real life example:
Payment completed.
User ka ₹10,000 transfer ho gaya.
App bolta hai "success".

Agar server crash ke baad record gayab ho gaya:
→ catastrophic failure
→ money inconsistency
→ trust loss

So we replicate.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WITHOUT REPLICATION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

User → Server A writes order
Server A disk corrupts
Data gone forever

Single machine durability weak hoti hai because:
- disk failure
- machine crash
- accidental deletion
- power issue
- cloud zone failure

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WITH REPLICATION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Write copied to:
- Node A
- Node B
- Node C

Even if A dies, B/C still preserve data.

Real systems:
- banking ledgers
- orders
- inventory
- user profiles
- chat messages
- social posts

Tradeoff:
Higher durability usually means:
- more network hops
- more write latency
- more storage cost

==============================================================================
2) THROUGHPUT — WHY REPLICATION IMPROVES SCALE
==============================================================================

Example:
Instagram-like app
- 10M users
- reads huge
- writes moderate

Single DB:
100K QPS max

Traffic:
500K read QPS

Single DB dead.

So replicas add karo.

1 primary + 5 read replicas
Now reads distribute:
500K / 5 = 100K each

Beautiful scale.

Interview point:
Replication especially boosts READ throughput.

Important:
Writes scale only in some replication models.
This is where Single Leader vs Multi Leader vs Leaderless matters.

==============================================================================
3) GEOGRAPHY / MULTI REGION
==============================================================================

User in India, US, Europe.

If all data only US me:
India user ko 250ms lag sakta hai.

If India replica:
local read latency maybe 20ms.

Real systems:
- Netflix user profiles
- YouTube metadata
- CDN configs
- Twitter timelines
- product catalog

Also DR:
US region down?
Serve from Europe.

Tradeoff:
Cross-region consistency harder.

==============================================================================
4) SINGLE LEADER REPLICATION
==============================================================================

This is the MOST COMMON interview default.

Other names:
- Primary Replica
- Master Slave
- Leader Follower

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARCHITECTURE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                 Writes
Client ───────────────► Leader
                         │
                         ├──── replicate log ───► Follower 1
                         ├──── replicate log ───► Follower 2
                         └──── replicate log ───► Follower 3

Reads:
- from leader
- or followers

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTUITION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ek class monitor hai.
Attendance register me sirf monitor likh sakta hai.
Baaki students us copy ko dekh sakte hain.

Only one source of truth for writes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WRITE FLOW
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) client sends write to leader
2) leader validates
3) writes WAL / binlog / commit log
4) applies locally
5) ships log to followers
6) followers replay same ordered writes
7) ack returned

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY SINGLE LEADER IS BEAUTIFUL
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Because ordering easy ho jati hai.

If writes:
A = 5
A = 10
A = 20

Leader ensures exact order preserved.

This dramatically simplifies:
- conflict resolution
- transactions
- constraints
- secondary indexes
- CDC
- cache invalidation

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Stronger consistency easier
Best for:
- payments
- orders
- booking
- inventory

2) Conflict free writes
Only one writer.

3) Easy debugging
All writes one path.

4) Transaction support easy
SQL databases mostly use this.

5) Easy replication log
binlog/WAL based.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Write bottleneck
All writes leader pe.

2) Leader SPOF (without failover)
If leader dies → no writes

3) Failover complexity
Election + promotion required

4) Cross-region write latency
Global writes one leader tak jayengi

5) Replica lag
Followers stale ho sakte hain

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL EXAMPLES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- PostgreSQL streaming replication
- MySQL replicas
- MongoDB primary-secondary
- Redis replica mode
- Kafka partition leader-follower

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHEN TO USE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Default HLD choice when:
- strong consistency important
- moderate write scale
- read heavy system
- transactional workload

Example:
Food delivery order system

Why?
Order duplication nahi chahiye.

==============================================================================
5) MULTI LEADER REPLICATION
==============================================================================

Here multiple nodes accept writes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARCHITECTURE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Region India  ◄────sync────► Region US ◄────sync────► Region Europe
   Leader A                      Leader B                 Leader C

Each region local writes accept karta hai.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTUITION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Google Docs analogy:
Multiple people edit from different locations.
Each local server accepts edits.
Then changes sync hote hain.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY MULTI LEADER?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Single leader global latency problem solve.

India user:
write India leader
No need US round trip.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL USE CASES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Multi-region apps
2) collaborative editing
3) offline-first apps
4) branch office systems
5) shopping cart sync
6) messaging draft sync

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Low write latency globally
Local region writes.

2) Better availability
One region down, others still writable.

3) Regional autonomy
Each region independent.

4) Good for async workflows

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BIGGEST PROBLEM = CONFLICTS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

India leader:
name = "Anubhav"

US leader same time:
name = "Anu"

Now sync?
Conflict.

This is the CORE tradeoff.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFLICT RESOLUTION STRATEGIES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Last Write Wins
Simple but dangerous.

2) Version vectors
Detect concurrent updates.

3) CRDTs
For counters, sets, collaborative docs.

4) App specific merge
Cart items union.
Profile fields merge by timestamp.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Conflict complexity huge
Most important interview point.

2) Debugging hard
Race across regions.

3) Causality issues
Ordering across leaders complex.

4) Transaction guarantees weaker
Especially global txns.

5) Higher operational complexity

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHEN TO USE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use when:
- global writes
- low latency writes needed
- conflict acceptable / resolvable

Examples:
- Google Docs
- Notion collaborative docs
- shopping cart sync
- social draft edits

==============================================================================
6) LEADERLESS REPLICATION
==============================================================================

This is Dynamo/Cassandra style.

No leader.
Any node can accept write.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARCHITECTURE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

          ┌──────── Node A
Client ───┼──────── Node B
          └──────── Node C

Write multiple replicas directly.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTUITION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Imagine 3 teachers maintain same attendance sheet.
You can submit update to any 2.
Later they reconcile.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WRITE FLOW
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Replication factor N = 3

Write to:
A, B, C

Success condition:
W = 2 acknowledgements enough

Even if C down:
A + B success => write accepted

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READ FLOW
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Read from R replicas.

Suppose:
A = value 10
B = value 10
C = value 8 (stale)

Read R=2:
choose latest by timestamp/version

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) No leader bottleneck
Great write scale.

2) No leader failover pain

3) High availability
Some nodes down still works.

4) Great for partition tolerance
Excellent distributed systems design.

5) Multi region friendly

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Conflict resolution needed
Concurrent writes common.

2) Read repair complexity

3) Anti entropy required
Merkle trees / background sync

4) Stale reads possible

5) Harder mental model

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL SYSTEMS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- DynamoDB core ideas
- Cassandra
- Riak
- Voldemort (old)
- ScyllaDB style concepts

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHEN TO USE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Excellent for:
- huge scale writes
- logs
- feeds
- counters
- IoT telemetry
- event ingestion
- social likes/views

Not ideal for:
- strict banking ledger

==============================================================================
7) QUORUMS — THE HEART OF LEADERLESS
==============================================================================

This is interviewer’s favorite.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEFINITIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

N = total replicas
W = write acknowledgements required
R = read replicas consulted

Condition:
R + W > N

Then read/write overlap guaranteed.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLASSIC EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

N = 3
W = 2
R = 2

Since:
2 + 2 > 3

At least one common node overlap karega.

That common node likely latest write rakhega.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THIS WORKS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Write goes to A,B
Read goes to B,C

Overlap = B

So latest value visible.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
POPULAR CONFIGURATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Fast writes
N=3 W=1 R=3
Pros: write fast
Cons: stale risk

2) Balanced
N=3 W=2 R=2
Most common

3) Stronger reads
N=5 W=3 R=3

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL LIFE TRADEOFF
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Higher W:
- better durability
- better consistency
- slower writes

Higher R:
- fresher reads
- slower reads
- higher network cost

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SLIPPERY EDGE CASES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Even quorum != absolute strong consistency always.

Because:
1) sloppy quorum
2) hinted handoff
3) clock skew
4) concurrent writes
5) network partition
6) read from non-overlapping replica sets
7) async repair

This is very strong interview point.

Say:
"Quorums improve probabilistic consistency, but practical systems may still
show stale or conflicting reads due to clock skew, sloppy quorums, or
concurrent writes."

==============================================================================
8) HOW TO CHOOSE IN HLD INTERVIEW
==============================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE A: PAYMENT SYSTEM
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Choose:
Single leader

Why:
- strong ordering
- transactions
- no conflicts

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE B: GLOBAL DOC EDITOR
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Choose:
Multi leader + CRDT

Why:
- local low latency writes
- conflict merge required

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE C: LIKE COUNTER / VIEW COUNT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Choose:
Leaderless

Why:
- massive write scale
- eventual consistency acceptable

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE D: PRODUCT CATALOG
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Choose:
Single leader write + many read replicas

Why:
- reads huge
- writes manageable

